{
  // Dependency vulnerability gate (audit-ci, Apache-2.0). Runs `pnpm audit` and
  // fails CI on HIGH or CRITICAL advisories; moderate/low are reported but do
  // not block. Run with: `pnpm exec audit-ci --config audit-ci.jsonc`.
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  "report-type": "important",
  // ⚠️ THIS DOES NOTHING IN THIS REPO, and said the opposite until 2026-09-04. It read
  // "Continue past registry hiccups rather than flaking the build on a network blip",
  // which is behaviour audit-ci has never had here: it retries only when the error
  // message matches `PARTIAL_RETRY_ERROR_MSG[packageManager]`, and upstream ships
  // `pnpm: []  // TODO: Identify retry-able error message for pnpm`
  // (audit-ci@7.1.0/dist/bin.cjs). We are a pnpm workspace, so `isRetryableMessage` is
  // always false and this counter is never consulted. `--pass-enoaudit` is gated on the
  // same flag and would be equally inert — so do NOT reach for it when the registry is
  // down, and do NOT raise this number expecting it to help.
  //
  // Kept at 3 rather than deleted: it costs nothing, it is correct for npm/yarn, and it
  // becomes live the moment upstream fills that array in. The real retry lives in the
  // workflow step (ci.yml / deploy-shrink.yml), scoped to `ERR_SOCKET_TIMEOUT` so it can
  // never turn a finding into a pass.
  "retry-count": 3,
  // Allowlist specific advisories that are unfixable / not exploitable in this
  // context, with a dated justification, e.g. "GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx".
  //
  // Entries are PATH-SCOPED ("<advisory>|<dependency path>") on purpose: the
  // advisory stays blocking for any path not listed here, so if one of these
  // packages ever reaches runtime code the gate fails again instead of staying
  // silently suppressed. Re-check each entry when its parent package is bumped.
  // FOUR ENTRIES since 2026-09-26: two extract-zip advisories x two routes (at the
  // bottom). Two from 2026-09-10 to 2026-09-26. One from
  // 2026-08-13, empty from 2026-08-12 before that; getting back to empty is the goal.
  //
  // Two GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (brace-expansion DoS) paths lived here from
  // 2026-07-27, correctly: at the time the only patched release was 5.0.8, there
  // was no patched 1.x or 2.x, and v5 is a breaking API change that crashes
  // minimatch@3/@8. Both paths were build/CI-time only and unreachable by any
  // visitor, so suppressing them was the right call.
  //
  // Removed 2026-08-04. Upstream shipped 1.1.18 and 2.1.4, so the entries stopped
  // being "unfixable" and became "unfixed" — a different thing. They are now
  // pinned by `brace-expansion@1` / `@2` / `@5` overrides in package.json, and
  // audit-ci itself flagged the allowlist as redundant ("Consider not
  // allowlisting paths…"), which is the signal the header below asks for.
  //
  // The same pass fixed three more advisories that appeared between 2026-08-01
  // and 2026-08-04 — undici, fast-uri and ip-address — all by override rather
  // than suppression, because all three had patched versions available.
  //
  // ── image-size, added 2026-08-08 ────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Three high advisories were published within five minutes of each other on
  // 2026-08-07 20:50–20:55 UTC and blocked the first deploy after them. nanoid
  // (GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8) was fixed by override — `nanoid: ^3.3.17` in
  // package.json — because 3.3.17 exists. The two below could not be:
  //
  // ⚠️ AND THAT NANOID FIX CAME UNDONE ON 2026-08-14, WITHOUT ANYONE TOUCHING A
  // DEPENDENCY. The advisory was UPDATED at 2026-08-13T15:43Z to widen its
  // vulnerable range from `<3.3.17` to `<3.3.18`, so the pin that resolved it
  // six days earlier became the vulnerable version. CI had passed on 9c155cf at
  // 10:54 that morning and failed on the very next push with no dependency
  // change in the diff — the `deploy` job is gated on `audit`, so a viewer-only
  // CSS change was blocked by a re-scoped advisory in `apps/cms > vitest > vite
  // > postcss > nanoid`.
  //
  // Two things worth carrying forward. **An advisory is not a fixed target**:
  // "we already fixed that one" is not a reason to skip reading the range. And
  // **`^3.3.17` already permitted 3.3.18** — the range was never the blocker,
  // the LOCKFILE was, because a frozen install has no reason to re-resolve. The
  // fix was `install --no-frozen-lockfile` after raising the floor to ^3.3.18,
  // which is 157h old and clears the 24h `minimumReleaseAge` cooldown.
  //
  //   npm audit reports `"patched_versions": "<0.0.0"` for BOTH, which is how it
  //   says NO FIXED RELEASE EXISTS. `vulnerable_versions` is `<=2.0.2` and 2.0.2
  //   is the current latest. Upgrading payload cannot help while its dependency
  //   itself has no patched version. This is "unfixable", not "unfixed" — the
  //   distinction the brace-expansion note above turns on.
  //
  // Why suppressing them is defensible here, measured rather than assumed:
  //   - Both are DENIAL OF SERVICE via infinite loop (CWE-835). No data exposure.
  //   - The loops are in the ICNS, JXL and HEIF parsers. Media.ts restricts
  //     uploads to model/gltf-binary, image/webp, image/avif, image/jpeg and
  //     image/png — none of the three affected formats. The one loose entry,
  //     application/octet-stream, is constrained to names ending `.glb` by
  //     mediaRules.ts (`if (mimeType === 'application/octet-stream' && !lower
  //     .endsWith('.glb'))` → rejected).
  //   - Uploading requires authentication: Media.ts has `create: isAdminOrEditor`.
  //     There is no anonymous path to the parser at all.
  //   - The CMS runs on Cloudflare Workers, which enforce a CPU limit per
  //     request. An infinite loop terminates the invocation; it cannot hang a
  //     long-lived process the way it would on a Node server.
  //
  // NOT claimed: that this is unreachable. image-size sniffs magic bytes rather
  // than trusting the declared MIME type, so a hostile file named `.glb` is not
  // proven to be rejected before it reaches the parser. The argument above is
  // authenticated-attacker-only + DoS-only + CPU-capped, not "impossible".
  //
  // RESOLVED 2026-08-12 by the review this note asked for. payload was bumped
  // 3.86.0 → 3.88.0, and 3.88.0 REPLACES the dependency outright rather than
  // patching it: `image-size@2.0.2` is removed and `image-dimensions@2.5.1`
  // (sindresorhus, zero runtime deps, no install hooks) takes its place —
  // Payload cites the same two advisories at dist/uploads/probeImageSize.js.
  // `image-size` no longer appears anywhere in pnpm-lock.yaml; before this bump
  // it did.
  //
  // So the "unfixable" reading was correct at the time and stopped being true
  // upstream, exactly like brace-expansion on 2026-08-04. Both lines deleted
  // after re-running the gate without them: `high: 0, critical: 0`, passed.
  // audit-ci had already emitted its own "Consider not allowlisting paths…"
  // hint, which is the signal the header above asks you to act on.
  //
  // Worth recording because the old note was honest about its own limit: it
  // explicitly did NOT claim unreachability, since image-size sniffs magic bytes
  // rather than trusting the declared MIME type, so a hostile file named `.glb`
  // was never proven to be rejected before reaching the parser. That residual
  // path is now gone rather than argued down.
  //
  // ── extract-zip, added 2026-08-13 ──────────────────────────────────────────
  // GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv, "unvalidated symlink path traversal", CWE-22, CVSS 8.1
  // (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N). Advisory published 2026-06-26, but
  // the gate passed on every run up to 2026-08-12 19:40 (bfaf84b) and failed on
  // the next one, 2026-08-13 07:18 (6f55166). NOTHING IN THIS REPO CHANGED to
  // cause it: `extract-zip` appears 4 times in pnpm-lock.yaml at BOTH commits,
  // and the only dependency edit between them was adding @vitest/coverage-v8.
  // What moved was npm's advisory data. Worth knowing before hunting a commit.
  //
  // UNFIXABLE, not unfixed — the distinction the brace-expansion and image-size
  // notes above both turn on. `extract-zip` latest IS 2.0.1, the advisory range
  // is `<=2.0.1`, and npm reports `"patched_versions": "<0.0.0"`. No override
  // can help, because there is nothing to override to.
  //
  // WHY THIS PATH IS SAFE TO SUPPRESS. @lhci/cli is a root devDependency — the
  // Lighthouse CI gate. It never ships to a visitor. The only archive
  // @puppeteer/browsers extracts is the Chrome build it downloads from Google's
  // CDN, so the hostile zip the advisory needs is not attacker-supplied here;
  // the CVSS vector agrees, requiring UI:R.
  //
  // ⚠️ THE SECOND PATH IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED — and as of 2026-08-18 there
  // is no second path at all. It used to read: a runtime route
  // @run-apparel/shrink (dependencies) → @cloudflare/puppeteer@1.3.0 →
  // @puppeteer/browsers@2.2.4 → extract-zip, which audit-ci did not report.
  // Leaving it unlisted was the point of path scoping — if it were ever
  // reported this gate would fail again rather than stay silently suppressed —
  // and that reasoning still stands for any future path. Do not "tidy up" by
  // adding one.
  //
  // ── RE-MEASURED 2026-08-18 (audit M1) ──────────────────────────────────────
  // Four factual claims in this note had gone stale, all at once, when the
  // `browser` binding was removed on 2026-08-17 with the poster capture (Browser
  // Rendering is billed per session-second). Measured, not inferred:
  //
  //   claim                                    | measured 2026-08-18
  //   -----------------------------------------|---------------------------
  //   bundle is 792,166 bytes                  | 77,955 (index.js);
  //                                            | 181,493 whole outdir
  //   puppeteer.launch(env.BROWSER) at         | that file is 755 lines;
  //   apps/shrink/src/index.ts:806             | no such call exists
  //   a runtime route to extract-zip exists    | `pnpm why extract-zip -r`
  //                                            | shows ONLY the @lhci/cli
  //                                            | devDependency route
  //   @cloudflare/puppeteer is a shrink dep    | absent from
  //                                            | apps/shrink/package.json
  //
  // THE CONCLUSION DID NOT WEAKEN — IT STRENGTHENED. The runtime path is GONE,
  // not merely unbundled. Re-verified by measurement: the dry-run bundle
  // contains ZERO occurrences of `extract-zip` or `@puppeteer/browsers`.
  //
  // Why this mattered enough to record rather than quietly correct: an engineer
  // following the RE-CHECK instruction below would have opened
  // apps/shrink/src/index.ts:806, found a 755-line file, and been unable to tell
  // whether the justification was wrong, the file was truncated, or the
  // suppression is unsafe. The correct conclusion — that the risk DECREASED —
  // is the one the stale evidence no longer supported on its face.
  //
  // RE-CHECK: whenever @lhci/cli, @cloudflare/puppeteer or puppeteer-core is
  // bumped, AND whenever a BINDING is added to or removed from
  // apps/shrink/wrangler.jsonc. That second trigger was added 2026-08-18 because
  // a binding removal is what actually invalidated every number above, and no
  // trigger here named it — the list said "dependency bumps" and the event was
  // not a dependency bump.
  //
  // DELETE THIS ENTRY WHEN: `pnpm why extract-zip -r` no longer reports it — ie
  // when @lhci/cli stops depending on it. Changed 2026-08-18 from "the day
  // extract-zip ships a patched release" (audit N5), which is a condition that
  // may never occur: extract-zip's latest is 2.0.1, published 2020-06-10, the
  // most recent of 24 versions, and CVE-2026-56876 reports patched_versions
  // "<0.0.0". The UNFIXABLE-not-unfixed reasoning above is confirmed still
  // correct; only the condition it waited on was unreachable.
  //
  // ── extract-zip, a SECOND advisory, added 2026-09-10 ────────────────────────
  // GHSA-7pqw-9j4j-h8q3 (CVE-2026-19693), "extract-zip allows arbitrary file writes through symlink archive entries", published 2026-08-17 — a
  // different advisory against the SAME package at the SAME dependency path as the
  // entry above. It blocked the first CI run of the public repository on 2026-09-09.
  // UNFIXABLE for the same measured reason: extract-zip's latest is still 2.0.1
  // and GitHub reports no first patched version. The safety argument is the one
  // recorded above, unchanged: @lhci/cli is a root devDependency, and the only
  // archive @puppeteer/browsers extracts is the Chrome build it downloads from
  // Google's CDN. DELETE WITH the entry above — both go the day
  // `pnpm why extract-zip -r` stops reporting this path.
  //
  // ── the same two advisories by a SECOND route, added 2026-09-26 ────────────
  // Nothing in the dependency tree changed; the reporter did. Moving pnpm
  // 10.34.5 → 12.6.0 left the lockfile's package section byte-identical, yet
  // `pnpm audit` now reports each advisory by TWO routes where pnpm 10 reported
  // one: the route above AND `@lhci/cli>@lhci/utils>lighthouse>…`. Measured the
  // same day on the same lockfile: pnpm 10 → "Passed", pnpm 12 → failed on the
  // @lhci/utils route alone. The route is real — the lockfile has
  // `@lhci/utils@0.15.1: dependencies: lighthouse: 12.6.1`, the SAME lighthouse
  // copy @lhci/cli uses — and it sits inside the same root devDependency, so
  // the safety argument above covers it unchanged. Listed path by path, like
  // the others, so any route outside @lhci/cli still fails the gate; the owner
  // chose this over an advisory-wide entry. DELETE WITH the entries above.
  "allowlist": [
    "GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv|@lhci/cli>lighthouse>puppeteer-core>@puppeteer/browsers>extract-zip",
    "GHSA-7pqw-9j4j-h8q3|@lhci/cli>lighthouse>puppeteer-core>@puppeteer/browsers>extract-zip",
    "GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv|@lhci/cli>@lhci/utils>lighthouse>puppeteer-core>@puppeteer/browsers>extract-zip",
    "GHSA-7pqw-9j4j-h8q3|@lhci/cli>@lhci/utils>lighthouse>puppeteer-core>@puppeteer/browsers>extract-zip"
  ]
}
